---
import bbsLogo from '../../../assets/BBS/BBS_Logo_Final.png';
import clientView from '../../../assets/BBS/BBS_Client_View2.png';
import clientCollapsed from '../../../assets/BBS/BBS_Client_View3.png';
import serverView from '../../../assets/BBS/BBS_Server_View2.png';
import pluginView from '../../../assets/BBS/BBS_Server_View3.png';
import launchVideo from '../../../assets/BBS/Start_to_Gridworld.mp4';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import '../../styles/site.css';

const technologies = ['Go', 'C# / Avalonia', 'JavaScript', 'SQLite', 'TCP/IP sockets', 'Distributed systems'];
const documentationPaths = [
  { number: '01', category: 'Start here', title: 'Onboarding', text: 'Getting Started and the Quickstart Checklist turn a multi-part system into a clear first run.', href: 'https://jonkirkpatrick.github.io/bbs/docs/category/onboarding' },
  { number: '02', category: 'Build with it', title: 'Guides', text: 'Task-focused walkthroughs show how to connect to a remote server and author a new plugin.', href: 'https://jonkirkpatrick.github.io/bbs/docs/category/guides' },
  { number: '03', category: 'Understand the boundaries', title: 'Architecture', text: "Architecture notes and decision records make the platform's responsibilities, tradeoffs, and boundaries legible.", href: 'https://jonkirkpatrick.github.io/bbs/docs/category/architecture' },
  { number: '04', category: 'Integrate and operate', title: 'Reference, deployment, and releases', text: 'Protocols, packaging notes, versioning, and the roadmap describe how the system can be used beyond a local demo.', href: 'https://jonkirkpatrick.github.io/bbs/docs' }
];
const gallery = [
  { image: clientView, label: 'Client orchestration', text: 'The client manages servers, bot sessions, arenas, and the flow of data between them.' },
  { image: serverView, label: 'Server state', text: 'A live arena exposes the state needed to understand what an environment and its agents are doing.' },
  { image: clientCollapsed, label: 'Observation mode', text: 'The interface can get out of the way when the goal is to observe a live environment or replay.' },
  { image: pluginView, label: 'Plugin boundary', text: 'Environment visuals are served by the plugin itself, keeping the core platform independent of the experiment.' }
];
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="Build-a-Bot Stadium, a modular platform for AI experimentation by Jon Kirkpatrick." />
    <title>Build-a-Bot Stadium | Jon Kirkpatrick</title>
  </head>
  <body class="case-study-page">
    <header class="site-header">
      <a class="wordmark" href="/" aria-label="Jon Kirkpatrick home">JK<span>.</span></a>
      <nav aria-label="Primary navigation">
        <a href="/#work">Selected work</a>
        <a href="/#about">About</a>
        <a class="nav-button" href="mailto:jon.kirkpatrick@pm.me">Get in touch</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="case-hero" aria-labelledby="case-title">
        <div class="case-hero-copy">
          <a class="back-link" href="/">← Back to selected work</a>
          <p class="eyebrow">01 / Distributed AI experimentation</p>
          <h1 id="case-title">Build-a-Bot<br /><em>Stadium</em></h1>
          <p class="case-lede">A modular client/server platform for running AI experiments across multiple environments without rebuilding the tooling every time.</p>
          <div class="hero-actions">
            <a class="button button-primary" href="https://github.com/JonKirkpatrick/bbs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View repository <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
            <a class="text-link" href="https://jonkirkpatrick.github.io/bbs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read documentation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="case-hero-mark">
          <Image src={bbsLogo} alt="Build-a-Bot Stadium logo" widths={[320, 640, 960]} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 80vw, 360px" />
          <span>Build environments.<br />Deploy agents.<br />Study behavior.</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="case-facts" aria-label="Project facts">
        <div><span>Role</span><strong>Design + engineering</strong></div>
        <div><span>Focus</span><strong>Modular experimentation</strong></div>
        <div><span>Status</span><strong>Early-stage / functional</strong></div>
        <div><span>Stack</span><strong>Go · C# · JavaScript</strong></div>
      </section>

      <section class="case-section case-introduction" aria-labelledby="challenge-title">
        <div class="case-section-label"><span>01</span><span>The challenge</span></div>
        <div class="case-section-body">
          <h2 id="challenge-title">Experimentation is hard enough without rebuilding the arena.</h2>
          <div class="case-two-column-copy">
            <p>AI and reinforcement-learning experiments tend to accumulate their own bespoke infrastructure. A new environment means another launch path, another client, another way to inspect state, and another collection of assumptions that quietly become part of the experiment.</p>
            <p>BBS began as an attempt to separate those concerns. The platform owns identity, orchestration, communication, and persistence. Individual environments can focus on being good environments.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="architecture-band" aria-labelledby="architecture-title">
        <div class="architecture-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">The central idea</p>
          <h2 id="architecture-title">Keep the arena stable.<br /><em>Let the experiments change.</em></h2>
          <p>The system is split into a Go server, a C# Avalonia client, and an extensible plugin boundary. They communicate through a lightweight JSONL-over-TCP protocol that keeps the moving pieces understandable.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="architecture-diagram" aria-label="BBS architecture flow">
          <div class="diagram-node diagram-client"><span>01</span><strong>Client</strong><small>Orchestrates sessions</small></div>
          <div class="diagram-connector">JSONL / TCP</div>
          <div class="diagram-node diagram-server"><span>02</span><strong>Server</strong><small>Hosts arenas</small></div>
          <div class="diagram-connector">Plugin API</div>
          <div class="diagram-node diagram-plugin"><span>03</span><strong>Process</strong><small>Owns the experiment</small></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="case-section documentation-section" aria-labelledby="documentation-title">
        <div class="case-section-label"><span>02</span><span>The system includes its own map</span></div>
        <div class="case-section-body">
          <h2 id="documentation-title">A platform is only useful when other people can find their way through it.</h2>
          <p class="section-intro">BBS is documented as a system that people can understand, operate, extend, and eventually contribute to. The documentation follows the same path as the software: from first launch, through experimentation, down to the contracts and decisions that keep the platform coherent.</p>
          <div class="document-grid">
            {documentationPaths.map((path) => (
              <article class="document-abstract">
                <span class="document-number">{path.number}</span>
                <p class="project-category">{path.category}</p>
                <h3>{path.title}</h3>
                <p>{path.text}</p>
                <a class="card-link" href={path.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Browse {path.title.toLowerCase()} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
              </article>
            ))}
          </div>
          <a class="text-link documentation-link" href="https://jonkirkpatrick.github.io/bbs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore the BBS documentation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="walkthrough-title">
        <div class="case-section-label"><span>03</span><span>In the system</span></div>
        <div class="case-section-body">
          <h2 id="walkthrough-title">A platform that stays out of the way.</h2>
          <div class="case-gallery">
            {gallery.map((item, index) => (
              <figure class:list={{ 'gallery-feature': index === 0 }}>
                <div class="gallery-image"><Image src={item.image} alt={item.label} widths={[480, 800, 1200]} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 800px" loading={index === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'} /></div>
                <figcaption><strong>{item.label}</strong><span>{item.text}</span></figcaption>
              </figure>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="demo-section" aria-labelledby="demo-title">
        <div class="demo-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">A working slice</p>
          <h2 id="demo-title">From launch to Gridworld.</h2>
          <p>The visuals are still deliberately developer-facing, but the sequence shows the important part: launch the client, connect to a remote server, deploy a bot, and enter a live environment.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href={launchVideo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open video file <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </div>
        <div class="demo-video-wrap">
          <video controls preload="metadata" poster={bbsLogo.src}>
            <source src={launchVideo} type="video/mp4" />
            Your browser does not support embedded video.
          </video>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="case-section case-outcome" aria-labelledby="outcome-title">
        <div class="case-section-label"><span>04</span><span>What I learned</span></div>
        <div class="case-section-body">
          <h2 id="outcome-title">The useful abstraction is the one that leaves room.</h2>
          <div class="case-two-column-copy">
            <p>The most important design decision was treating the environment as a first-class plugin rather than a feature of the client. That keeps the platform’s responsibilities legible and gives an environment author control over the visual experience.</p>
            <p>There is still plenty to refine: the client needs a richer visual language, the protocol will evolve as more environments arrive, and the server needs the hardening that comes with real use. But the foundation is functional and pointed in the right direction.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="tag-list case-tags" aria-label="BBS technologies">
            {technologies.map((technology) => <span>{technology}</span>)}
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <span>Jon Kirkpatrick</span>
      <span>Build-a-Bot Stadium</span>
      <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/jonathan-kirkpatrick-62b428330" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn ↗</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/JonKirkpatrick" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub ↗</a>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
